# Value vs Pointer Semantics in Go

One of the most important design decisions in Go is choosing between **value semantics** (copy of data) and **pointer semantics** (reference to data). The right choice affects both correctness and performance.

## Value Semantics — Data Copy

```go
type Point struct{ X, Y int }

func moveByValue(p Point, dx, dy int) Point {
    p.X += dx // modifies copy, not original
    p.Y += dy
    return p
}

original := Point{1, 2}
moved := moveByValue(original, 5, 3)
fmt.Println(original) // {1 2} — unchanged
fmt.Println(moved)    // {6 5}
```

## Pointer Semantics — Data Reference

```go
func moveByPointer(p *Point, dx, dy int) {
    p.X += dx // modifies original!
    p.Y += dy
}

original := Point{1, 2}
moveByPointer(&original, 5, 3)
fmt.Println(original) // {6 5} — changed!
```

## Performance Consideration

```go
type LargeStruct struct { Data [1000]int; Name string }

// Expensive — copies 1000 ints each call
func processByValue(s LargeStruct) { fmt.Println(s.Name) }

// Efficient — copies only 8-byte pointer
func processByPointer(s *LargeStruct) { fmt.Println(s.Name) }
```

## Escape Analysis

Go compiler automatically determines stack vs heap placement:
```
go build -gcflags='-m' .
// ./main.go:3:2: moved to heap: x
```

